# What a WHOLE-TREE prettier glob must not rewrite.
#
# The emitted `format` / `format:check` scripts are one `**/*.{ext,...}` glob rather than a list of
# path patterns, so a directory added to this template is read by the formatter without anyone
# remembering to widen a list. This file is the other half of that, and exactly ONE line in it is
# load-bearing today.
#
# MEASURED, on a copy of an emitted tree carrying a built `dist/`, a `coverage/`, an installed
# `node_modules/`, a `.pnpm-store/` and a `pnpm-lock.yaml`: with this file absent the whole-tree
# glob reaches `pnpm-lock.yaml` and nothing else it should not. Prettier's default `--ignore-path`
# is `[.gitignore, .prettierignore]` and the emitted `.gitignore` already carries every generated
# and installed directory below. A lockfile is the one tool-owned file that is COMMITTED, so it is
# the one a `.gitignore` can never cover, and reformatting it churns it on every `pnpm install`.
#
# The rest is stated rather than relied on: it says out loud what must never be reformatted if the
# emitted `.gitignore` is ever narrowed. `.git/` is documentary too - prettier hard-skips `.git`,
# measured by planting files inside it. The set is `hl7`'s plus `.pnpm-store/`; the other parser
# repos that carry one do NOT agree with each other, and most carry none, so this is a choice of
# reference rather than a fleet convention.

# Lockfiles: tool-owned, committed, rewritten by the package manager on every install.
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json
yarn.lock

# Generated artifacts (also in .gitignore)
dist/
coverage/

# Dependencies (also in .gitignore; prettier additionally skips node_modules by default)
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Git / VCS internals
.git/
